description:
FOUND s497 (Fable, 2026-09-26) reviewing s494g (#2107), on f6a2348b.  On the base (3e8bff3e) the same program died earlier (by USR1's default action), so the hang is only REACHABLE since #2107 -- not a regression of a passing behaviour, but a new worst case: a hang where perl exits 0.  Probe `~/pcl-agent-scratch/s497/review/s494g/r6-string-handler-pkg.pl`:

    package Foo; sub h { print "Foo::h got $_[0]\n" } $SIG{USR1} = 'h'; kill 'USR1', $$; sleep 1;
    package main; sub g { print "main::g got $_[0]\n" } $SIG{USR2} = 'g'; kill 'USR2', $$; sleep 1; print "done\n";
  perl:  (USR1: handler main::h undefined -> ignored)  main::g got USR2 / done, exit 0
  PCL:   stderr `PCL: %SIG: $SIG{USR2} is not implemented -- the SBCL runtime uses this signal itself; the handler is NOT installed`, then `kill 'USR2', $$` and the process SURVIVES 15 s until KILLed (no stdout at all).

#2107 derives the deny list at boot (ILL TRAP ABRT BUS FPE SEGV USR2 URG on SBCL 2.6/Linux) and announces a handler store; it does not guard `kill`.  Delivering SIGUSR2 to an SBCL thread that is not waiting for a GC stop hangs it.  FIX SHAPE: `p-kill` (and the `kill 0`-style probes are unaffected) checks the target pid == $$ (or the process group containing it) against the deny list and DIES one line (`Signal USR2 is used by the runtime and cannot be sent to this process`), trappable; an EXTERNAL `kill -USR2` stays documented in not-supported.md's %SIG entry (it hangs any SBCL).  Rule 12's boundary: an effect-only miss may announce, but a HANG is not "continues correctly".  Guard: a row in Pl/t/signals-01.t (dies, stdout as perl minus the USR2 line).  Family: #2107, #2265.
